Let $K$ be a number field, fix integers $B\geq 1$ and $d\geq 2$. A generalized Hénon map of degree $d$ over $K$ is an automorphism $f:\mathbb{A}^2\to\mathbb{A}^2$ of the form $f=f_1\circ f_2\circ\cdots\circ f_m$, where each $f_i(x,y)=(y,\,p_i(y)-a_i x)$ with $p_i\in K[y]$ of degree $d_i\geq 2$, $a_i\in K^\times$, and $d=d_1d_2\cdots d_m$. Associated to such an $f$ are:
\begin{itemize}
\item the canonical height $\hat h_f:\mathbb{A}^2(\overline K)\to[0,\infty)$, characterized (up to standard normalizations) by $\hat h_f = \hat h_f^+ + \hat h_f^-$ where $\hat h_f^\pm(P)=\lim_{n\to\infty}d^{-n}h^+(f^{\pm n}P)$ for a suitable Weil-type height $h^+$ on $\mathbb{A}^2$;
\item the height $h(f)$ of $f$, defined as the Weil height of the tuple of coefficients of $f$ (viewed as a point in a suitable projective space of coefficients).
\end{itemize}

Question: Do there exist constants $\varepsilon>0$ and $C\geq 0$, depending only on $K$, $B$, and $d$, such that for every generalized Hénon map $f$ of degree $d$ defined over $K$, the set
$$\bigl\{P\in\mathbb{A}^2(\overline K)\;:\;[K(P):K]\leq B \text{ and }\hat h_f(P)<\varepsilon\, h(f)+C\bigr\}$$
has cardinality bounded above by a constant depending only on $K$, $B$, and $d$ (and not on $f$)?

